WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.585
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Well today I'm

00:00:01.585 --> 00:00:04.140
invited by xMatters
in their kitchen,

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:06.600
and Travis DePuy
is going to walk me

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:09.870
through how they've been through
this journey of migrating

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:14.430
from monolithic application into
microservices on Google Cloud

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:15.407
Platform.

00:00:15.407 --> 00:00:18.389
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:26.530
Thank you, Travis,
for having me over.

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:27.420
TRAVIS DEPUY: Thanks for coming.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:27.790
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Awesome.

00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:30.370
Well, I'm super excited to
actually cook with you today

00:00:30.370 --> 00:00:33.200
and to eat, because
that's what I like to do.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:34.870
But before we do
that, I would love

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:37.960
to learn more about xMatters
and what the tool does.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:39.940
TRAVIS DEPUY: We help
keep digital services up

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:40.540
and running.

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:43.150
We do that by tracking down
the current on call resources,

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:44.890
delivering
notifications to them,

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:46.840
and letting them
initiate workflow.

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:49.150
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So what
the tool basically does is,

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:52.570
it takes the times when there's
spikes in traffic or spikes

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:55.750
in error rates, and then it
notifies the right teams.

00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:57.940
But that stuff is
really hard to do.

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:00.160
How do you understand
all of that--

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:01.478
and also very complicated?

00:01:01.478 --> 00:01:02.270
TRAVIS DEPUY: Yeah.

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:06.020
Yeah, it is hard, and
important critical work

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:07.900
for the industries today.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:10.150
And there's, once you get
the notifications, you know,

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:12.130
that's just the
beginning of the process.

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:14.842
There's a lot steps that
have to happen after that.

00:01:14.842 --> 00:01:15.925
And so that's called toil.

00:01:15.925 --> 00:01:19.310
You know, important manual
work that needs to be done,

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:21.660
but it takes away time from
troubleshooting the issue,

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:24.635
so we can help automate
a lot of that process.

00:01:24.635 --> 00:01:27.010
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: This sounds,
obviously, very exciting.

00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:28.538
I'm interested in
seeing the tool.

00:01:28.538 --> 00:01:29.830
Can you show me how this works?

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:30.400
TRAVIS DEPUY: Yeah, sure.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:31.610
I have a computer right here.

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:33.780
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Awesome.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:36.280
TRAVIS DEPUY: Here we see part
of the xMatters flow designer

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:39.020
canvas showing a
Stackdriver alarm workflow.

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:41.050
The first step is the
inbound HDB trigger

00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:43.840
that parses the payload
and kicks things off.

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:46.300
Then we start enriching the
alarm with some metadata

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:48.515
that Stackdriver might not have.

00:01:48.515 --> 00:01:51.140
In this case, we're getting the
last commit and deployment info

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:52.707
from GitHub and Jenkins.

00:01:52.707 --> 00:01:54.290
And finally, this
is where we actually

00:01:54.290 --> 00:01:57.950
fire the xMatters
event to notify people.

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:00.440
Here we see Dan receiving
the Stackdriver notification

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:01.880
on his mobile device.

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:04.220
He reviews the stock
info from Stackdriver,

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:05.810
but then he can
review the enriched

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:07.323
from GitHub and Jenkins.

00:02:07.323 --> 00:02:09.240
He has a couple of
different response options,

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:10.550
but he decided this
is severe enough

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:12.592
that he needs to roll back
to the blue deployment

00:02:12.592 --> 00:02:14.900
from the current
green deployment.

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:18.422
So he selects a response option
and adds an optional comment.

00:02:18.422 --> 00:02:20.630
Back in the xMatters campus,
we see the various flows

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:23.180
that kick off when a user
selects the relevant response

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:24.140
option.

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:27.500
Each organization has major
incidents and processes

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:28.873
around kicking those off.

00:02:28.873 --> 00:02:30.290
In this case, we
see that xMatters

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:33.290
will automate creating a juror
issue, and then a status page

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:35.480
incident, and finally, a
Slack channel so that teams

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:37.360
can start collaborating.

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:39.405
We also see a rollback to
blue that Dan selected,

00:02:39.405 --> 00:02:40.780
and we see that
a new juror issue

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:44.010
is created before triggering
the redeploy using a Kubernetes

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:45.290
command.

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:48.130
The juror issue helps keep track
of who triggered the deployment

00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:49.750
and when, and also
serves as a record

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:51.780
for future investigation.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:53.280
The last one is my
favorite, though.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:55.070
Using a playbook
tool such as Ansible,

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:56.810
we can go trigger
an existing playbook

00:02:56.810 --> 00:02:59.180
to attempt to
remediate the problem.

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:00.800
There are limitless
possibilities,

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:03.290
and all this can be tailored
in the specific processes

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:04.623
of the organization.

00:03:04.623 --> 00:03:07.040
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: I can imagine
how this application must

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:09.140
have looked originally.

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:12.590
So I am super excited to
learn how you guys managed

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:16.010
to take all these services and
separate them out and create

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:17.370
microservices out of them.

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:20.338
But before we do that, I want to
know what we're cooking today.

00:03:20.338 --> 00:03:22.130
TRAVIS DEPUY: We're
cooking vegetable soup,

00:03:22.130 --> 00:03:23.610
and I think it's going
to be pretty tasty.

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:25.777
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: OK,
Travis, so where do we start?

00:03:25.777 --> 00:03:28.388
I am super excited looking
at all these veggies.

00:03:28.388 --> 00:03:29.180
TRAVIS DEPUY: Yeah.

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:30.513
We're going to chop them all up.

00:03:30.513 --> 00:03:33.620
So we've got some
celery, some parsley.

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:35.472
We've got some carrots
and some cheese.

00:03:35.472 --> 00:03:37.430
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Corns
and colorful tomatoes?

00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:38.940
Oh, good start.

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:39.440
OK.

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:41.482
So Travis, what do you
think about the difference

00:03:41.482 --> 00:03:43.810
between monoliths
and microservices?

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:45.413
How do you define it?

00:03:45.413 --> 00:03:48.080
TRAVIS DEPUY: Well, if you think
of a monolith like a turducken,

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:49.663
you know, that meat
monstrosity that's

00:03:49.663 --> 00:03:53.660
the chicken inside of the
duck inside of the turkey?

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:57.080
A lot like a turducken,
monoliths are very intertwined.

00:03:57.080 --> 00:04:01.820
Everything's all put together,
and rolling out new turduckens

00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:02.540
is very hard.

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:03.895
You have to rebuild each one.

00:04:03.895 --> 00:04:05.270
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Just like when

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:07.790
you're trying to build
something and you

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:10.050
have all the things
connected to each other,

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:12.208
and they're dependent
on each other,

00:04:12.208 --> 00:04:14.000
and you don't know
which one to take apart,

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:16.310
because the other
things might mess up.

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:17.240
TRAVIS DEPUY: Exactly.

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:17.610
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Got it.

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:18.545
OK.

00:04:18.545 --> 00:04:20.170
TRAVIS DEPUY:
Conversely, microservices

00:04:20.170 --> 00:04:21.470
are a lot like cupcakes, right?

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:23.920
If you have multiple
flavors of cupcakes

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:26.180
and you want to change one
little thing in the recipe,

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:29.463
you can then change the recipe,
bake a bunch of new cupcakes,

00:04:29.463 --> 00:04:31.880
and your customers get to have
all the greatest and latest

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:32.395
cupcakes.

00:04:32.395 --> 00:04:33.770
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Well, that was

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:36.320
the most delicious
explanation of the difference

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:38.420
between monoliths
and microservices

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:39.520
that I have heard.

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:42.260
So Travis, what were some
of the main challenges

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:44.510
that you all were
going through while you

00:04:44.510 --> 00:04:50.180
had this monolith turducken
setting of the architecture?

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:51.950
I'm pretty sure
there were challenges

00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:55.370
that made you think that
moving to microservices

00:04:55.370 --> 00:04:57.360
was a good choice.

00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:59.400
What were some of
those challenges?

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:02.450
TRAVIS DEPUY: Well, we had
monitoring and debugging

00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:03.020
challenges.

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:07.190
So each customer essentially
ended up in their own instance.

00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:09.860
And so trying to
track down issues

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:12.320
and trying to tell what the
health of the applications

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:14.900
were was really
difficult. We also found

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:16.880
that it was taking
days, if not weeks,

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:20.028
to get new customers onboarded,
which certainly isn't scalable.

00:05:20.028 --> 00:05:22.070
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: I'm
actually curious about how

00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:25.108
the infrastructure was initially
when it was a monolith.

00:05:25.108 --> 00:05:25.900
TRAVIS DEPUY: Yeah.

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:29.083
So originally, we had CDs that
we would send out to customers.

00:05:29.083 --> 00:05:31.250
We were, you know, it was
an on-premise application.

00:05:31.250 --> 00:05:33.890
So we just had to take that
and just put it into the cloud,

00:05:33.890 --> 00:05:35.263
naturally.

00:05:35.263 --> 00:05:36.680
And unfortunately,
what this meant

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:40.490
is that we ballooned into
5,000 different VMs, which,

00:05:40.490 --> 00:05:42.080
as I mentioned, isn't scalable.

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:43.080
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yeah.

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:46.490
So as you start to have 5,000
VMs, that definitely is a lot.

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:48.560
I'm assuming you
started to think

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:52.550
you needed to move to Cloud.

00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:54.008
What was that
thought process like?

00:05:54.008 --> 00:05:56.133
TRAVIS DEPUY: We rented
space in six different data

00:05:56.133 --> 00:05:57.230
centers across the world.

00:05:57.230 --> 00:06:00.770
So we thought about,
why are we getting

00:06:00.770 --> 00:06:02.130
into dealing with hardware.

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:02.630
You know?

00:06:02.630 --> 00:06:04.050
Google does that
very, very well.

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:06.265
So why don't we leave
it to the experts?

00:06:06.265 --> 00:06:07.640
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
What were some

00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:09.740
of the architectural
components at a high level

00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:11.390
that you were
looking at as a part

00:06:11.390 --> 00:06:12.818
of microservices architecture?

00:06:12.818 --> 00:06:13.610
TRAVIS DEPUY: Yeah.

00:06:13.610 --> 00:06:16.553
So naturally, the
application was composed

00:06:16.553 --> 00:06:17.720
of several different pieces.

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:19.850
So we had the web
user interface,

00:06:19.850 --> 00:06:23.990
we had API interfaces,
and we also

00:06:23.990 --> 00:06:26.780
have notification services,
so being able to reach out

00:06:26.780 --> 00:06:29.870
to email, or SMS, or
voice calls were all

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:31.700
their own kind of service.

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:34.160
Then there was also
the on-call scheduling,

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:37.100
so trying to figure out
who's currently on call.

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:38.885
And so these were kind
of the early things

00:06:38.885 --> 00:06:41.510
we were able to identify that we
could pull apart and tease out

00:06:41.510 --> 00:06:43.593
of the application to make
them their own service.

00:06:43.593 --> 00:06:45.093
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
When you all were

00:06:45.093 --> 00:06:47.120
starting to move
to microservices,

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:49.440
what was the end goal in mind?

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:50.690
TRAVIS DEPUY: Primarily scale.

00:06:50.690 --> 00:06:53.422
Maintaining 5,000 VMs
is just not scalable.

00:06:53.422 --> 00:06:55.130
So we tried to identify
different aspects

00:06:55.130 --> 00:06:57.047
that we could pull out
some of the redundancy.

00:06:57.047 --> 00:06:59.290
So, for example, we have
this application gateway

00:06:59.290 --> 00:07:01.740
that's speeding traffic
into the different services.

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:04.370
So we're able to identify
different places that we can

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:06.463
start to move to microservices.

00:07:06.463 --> 00:07:07.880
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
And then so how

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:09.812
did that start to
look in Google Cloud

00:07:09.812 --> 00:07:11.270
from the architectural
perspective?

00:07:11.270 --> 00:07:12.940
What are the components
you were using?

00:07:12.940 --> 00:07:13.732
TRAVIS DEPUY: Yeah.

00:07:13.732 --> 00:07:15.350
So we had a web user interface.

00:07:15.350 --> 00:07:17.750
We had application APIs.

00:07:17.750 --> 00:07:21.050
We had notification services
and on-call scheduling.

00:07:21.050 --> 00:07:22.820
So all these were
different microservices

00:07:22.820 --> 00:07:25.460
that we were able to
pull out and break off

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:26.730
into their own microservice.

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:29.150
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So
application gateway approach

00:07:29.150 --> 00:07:33.140
really sounds interesting
and the natural thing

00:07:33.140 --> 00:07:37.760
to do when you're starting out
with microservices, breaking up

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:39.170
your monolith.

00:07:39.170 --> 00:07:42.740
What are the components that you
ended up using on Google Cloud?

00:07:42.740 --> 00:07:44.760
What did the
architecture look like?

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:47.478
TRAVIS DEPUY: So we used Google
with DNS for IP address lookup.

00:07:47.478 --> 00:07:49.520
And then users are routed
through the global load

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:54.140
balancer, which gets them
into the application gateway.

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:58.190
So from there, the user devices,
such as web and mobile devices,

00:07:58.190 --> 00:08:01.280
are routed into the various
microservices that are all

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:05.030
running on GKE, using images
stored in Google Container

00:08:05.030 --> 00:08:05.750
Registry.

00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:08.310
The data backend is running
on Google Compute Engine.

00:08:08.310 --> 00:08:10.340
Then we have the
monitoring running, also

00:08:10.340 --> 00:08:13.950
using Prometheus, Splunk,
and, of course, Stackdriver.

00:08:13.950 --> 00:08:17.870
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Now, when you
decided to actually get the GKE

00:08:17.870 --> 00:08:20.690
services on, what were
some of the services

00:08:20.690 --> 00:08:25.040
that you picked out or
selected from the monolith that

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:26.390
were going to go on.

00:08:26.390 --> 00:08:28.800
What was the decision
making process around that,

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:30.342
and what were some
of those services?

00:08:30.342 --> 00:08:31.800
TRAVIS DEPUY: So
the application is

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:33.590
composed of many
different pieces,

00:08:33.590 --> 00:08:36.169
so it made logical sense to pull
those different pieces apart

00:08:36.169 --> 00:08:37.380
into their own services.

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:39.440
So, for example, we have
the web user interface.

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:43.620
It provides the web interface
for the various users.

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:46.570
Then we have the API
interfaces, as well as, I

00:08:46.570 --> 00:08:47.613
like on-call scheduling.

00:08:47.613 --> 00:08:50.030
So it's its own service to
figure out, OK, who's currently

00:08:50.030 --> 00:08:52.672
on call when an event comes in.

00:08:52.672 --> 00:08:54.130
And then there's
the data services.

00:08:54.130 --> 00:08:56.530
You know, keeping track of
users and their devices,

00:08:56.530 --> 00:08:57.540
and things like that.

00:08:57.540 --> 00:08:59.832
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So that
brings up a very interesting

00:08:59.832 --> 00:09:02.840
and a very important point when
it comes to migrating monoliths

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:07.430
to microservices, because
data dependencies are key.

00:09:07.430 --> 00:09:11.000
And every service has
some sort of dependency

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:12.440
on another service.

00:09:12.440 --> 00:09:15.200
So this question
always comes up,

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:19.850
as to how do you actually
define which services should

00:09:19.850 --> 00:09:23.420
be migrated first,
or how do you deal

00:09:23.420 --> 00:09:25.302
with those data dependencies?

00:09:25.302 --> 00:09:26.510
TRAVIS DEPUY: Yes, certainly.

00:09:26.510 --> 00:09:28.177
Microservices, you
know, are very rarely

00:09:28.177 --> 00:09:29.550
just standalone services.

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:31.700
You know, they always
depend on other pieces.

00:09:31.700 --> 00:09:34.370
So we just really made sure to
release the services in cadence

00:09:34.370 --> 00:09:38.180
with each other as we started
to build out the application.

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:40.610
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yeah,
so mainly just making sure

00:09:40.610 --> 00:09:44.000
that you list out
those services that you

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:45.680
may want to move
to microservices,

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:47.840
and then you define
the dependencies,

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:51.200
and then depending on which
one's dependent on each other,

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:52.880
or less dependent
on each other, you

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:55.100
define which one goes
first versus next.

00:09:55.100 --> 00:09:55.850
TRAVIS DEPUY: Yep.

00:09:55.850 --> 00:09:57.720
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: OK, well
that makes a lot of sense.

00:09:57.720 --> 00:09:59.512
So we have a bunch of
chopped veggies here.

00:09:59.512 --> 00:10:00.620
What do we do next?

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:01.550
TRAVIS DEPUY: We're going
to throw them in the pan

00:10:01.550 --> 00:10:02.730
and then we'll add in the broth.

